# AIF-C01 Full-Length Mock Exam

The five domain guides ([1](domain-1-fundamentals-of-ai-and-ml.md),
[2](domain-2-fundamentals-of-generative-ai.md),
[3](domain-3-applications-of-foundation-models.md),
[4](domain-4-guidelines-for-responsible-ai.md),
[5](domain-5-security-compliance-governance.md)) each carry 15–20
domain-siloed practice questions — good for checking whether you've learned
a single domain, but the real AWS Certified AI Practitioner (AIF-C01) exam
never asks questions one domain at a time. It mixes all five domains
together, weights them unevenly, and gives you a single clock running
against the whole set. This page is that rehearsal: **one continuous,
65-question exam** built to the same length, domain-weight distribution,
and time budget as the real thing.

Take it in one sitting, under a timer, before you sit the real exam. Don't
peek at the answer key until you've answered every question.

## How this mirrors the real exam

| | Real AIF-C01 exam | This mock exam |
|---|---|---|
| Question count | 65 (50 scored + 15 unscored, indistinguishable) | 65 |
| Format | Multiple choice (one correct answer) and multiple response (choose two) | Same mix — 3 multiple-response questions included |
| Time limit | 90 minutes | 90 minutes |
| Domain order | Domains interleaved randomly, not grouped | Domains interleaved (not grouped) — see the scoring key for which domain each question tests |
| Scoring | Scaled 100–1000, pass at 700 | Simple raw score out of 65, plus a per-domain breakdown (see [Scoring your results](#scoring-your-results)) |
| Reference material | None allowed | None — no domain guides, no notes |

### Domain weight distribution

The real exam draws questions from five domains at fixed proportions. This
mock exam uses the same proportions, applied to 65 questions:

| # | Domain | Exam weight | Questions in this mock exam |
|---|--------|-------------|------------------------------|
| 1 | Fundamentals of AI and ML | ~20% | 13 |
| 2 | Fundamentals of Generative AI | ~24% | 16 |
| 3 | Applications of Foundation Models | ~28% | 18 |
| 4 | Guidelines for Responsible AI | ~14% | 9 |
| 5 | Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI Solutions | ~14% | 9 |
| | **Total** | **100%** | **65** |

## Timing guidance

90 minutes for 65 questions is **83 seconds per question on average** — but
not every question deserves the same slice of that budget. Pace yourself
like this:

- **First pass (~60 minutes):** Move through all 65 questions in order.
  Spend no more than ~60–75 seconds on any single question on this pass.
  If a question isn't clicking within that window, make your best guess,
  flag it mentally (or on scratch paper: write down its number), and move
  on. A question you're stuck on is not worth more than a question you
  haven't seen yet.
- **Second pass (~20 minutes):** Return to the questions you flagged.
  Fresh eyes after a first pass often spot the distractor you missed the
  first time.
- **Final review (~10 minutes):** Skim every answer once more. Watch
  specifically for questions with "LEAST," "NOT," or "MOST cost-effective"
  in the stem — these are the ones test-takers misread under time pressure.
- **Multiple-response questions** ("Select TWO") take longer than
  single-answer questions because you must evaluate every option
  independently rather than stopping once you find the right one. Budget
  extra time for the three flagged in this exam.
- Do not leave any question blank. There is no penalty for guessing, so an
  educated guess always beats an omission.

## Instructions

1. Set a timer for 90 minutes before reading Question 1.
2. Answer all 65 questions below without consulting the domain guides,
   glossary, or notes.
3. Record your answers separately (letter per question number) so you can
   self-grade without seeing the correct answers while you work.
4. When the timer ends (or you finish early), stop and check your answers
   against the [answer key](#answer-key-and-explanations).
5. Use [Scoring your results](#scoring-your-results) to find your weakest
   domain and go back to that domain's guide before your next attempt.

---

## Mock exam questions

1. A company is building a system that must generate original marketing
   copy, product images, and short video clips from a text prompt, rather
   than classify or predict from existing labeled examples. Which
   category of AI best describes this system?
   A. Discriminative AI
   B. Generative AI
   C. Reinforcement learning
   D. Rule-based expert system

2. Which of the following is the best definition of a "token" in the
   context of large language models?
   A. A cryptographic key used to authenticate API calls to a model endpoint
   B. A unit of text — a word, part of a word, or punctuation mark — that a model processes as a discrete piece of input or output
   C. A single row in a training dataset used during fine-tuning
   D. A numeric score representing how confident a model is in its output

3. A retailer has no predefined categories for its customers and wants an
   algorithm to discover natural groupings in purchasing behavior on its
   own. Which type of machine learning fits this need?
   A. Supervised learning
   B. Unsupervised learning
   C. Reinforcement learning
   D. Semi-supervised learning

4. A company wants to let its support agents ask natural-language
   questions and get answers grounded in its private product manuals,
   without retraining or fine-tuning any model. Which approach is the
   best fit?
   A. Continued pre-training on the manuals
   B. Full fine-tuning of a foundation model on the manuals
   C. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) using the manuals as a knowledge base
   D. Increasing the model's temperature parameter

5. Which responsible AI dimension is most directly concerned with a
   stakeholder's ability to understand *why* a model produced a specific
   output, not merely whether the output is accurate?
   A. Robustness
   B. Explainability
   C. Scalability
   D. Latency

6. A company deploying a foundation model through Amazon Bedrock wants to
   ensure that credentials used to invoke the model carry only the
   permissions strictly needed for that task, and nothing more. Which AWS
   security principle does this describe?
   A. Shared responsibility model
   B. Least privilege
   C. Defense in depth
   D. Data residency

7. Which of the following is a well-known disadvantage of generative AI
   models that engineering teams must design around, even when a model is
   otherwise performing well?
   A. Inability to process any text input longer than one sentence
   B. Hallucination — producing plausible-sounding but factually incorrect output
   C. Requirement that all training data be labeled by hand
   D. Inability to run in a cloud environment

8. A data scientist trains a model that scores 97% accuracy on the
   training set but only 58% on a held-out test set. What does this
   pattern most likely indicate?
   A. Underfitting
   B. Overfitting
   C. Insufficient compute at inference time
   D. A mislabeled test set

9. A company wants to give business users a way to prototype generative
   AI ideas visually, without writing code or provisioning infrastructure.
   Which AWS offering is purpose-built for this?
   A. Amazon SageMaker Studio
   B. AWS Lambda
   C. PartyRock, an Amazon Bedrock Playground
   D. Amazon EC2

10. Numeric representations of words, sentences, or images that place
    semantically similar items close together in a high-dimensional space
    are called:
    A. Tokens
    B. Embeddings
    C. Hyperparameters
    D. Checkpoints

11. A company has a proprietary vocabulary and writing style it wants a
    foundation model to permanently absorb through further, unsupervised
    training on a large corpus of unlabeled domain text, without changing
    the base model's overall task capabilities. Which customization
    technique fits best?
    A. Prompt engineering
    B. Continued pre-training
    C. Retrieval-Augmented Generation
    D. Zero-shot prompting

12. A company's hiring model was trained on historical hiring data that
    reflected past inequities, and the model has learned to replicate
    those patterns. Which type of bias does this describe?
    A. Sampling bias
    B. Measurement bias
    C. Historical bias
    D. Aggregation bias

13. Which AWS service provides independently audited compliance reports
    (such as SOC, PCI DSS, and ISO certifications) that customers can
    download to support their own regulatory audits?
    A. AWS Artifact
    B. AWS CloudTrail
    C. AWS Config
    D. Amazon Macie

14. Which of the following best describes the relationship between
    artificial intelligence, machine learning, and deep learning?
    A. They are three unrelated, independently developed fields
    B. Deep learning is the broadest field, containing machine learning, which contains AI
    C. AI is the broadest field; machine learning is a subset of AI; deep learning is a subset of machine learning
    D. Machine learning and deep learning are two names for the same technique

15. A prompt engineer includes several example input/output pairs directly
    in the prompt before asking the model to complete a new, similar task.
    What is this technique called?
    A. Zero-shot prompting
    B. Few-shot prompting
    C. Negative prompting
    D. Retrieval-augmented prompting

16. A company evaluating foundation models for a customer-facing chatbot
    needs a model that responds within 200 milliseconds and fits a tight
    per-token cost budget, even if it means slightly lower output quality
    than the largest available model. Which factors should most directly
    drive the model selection decision?
    A. The model's training data cutoff date only
    B. Latency and cost, weighed against acceptable quality
    C. The model provider's headquarters location
    D. The number of parameters alone, regardless of latency or cost

17. Which AWS service is purpose-built to detect fraudulent online
    activity, such as payment fraud or fake account creation, using
    managed machine learning models with minimal setup?
    A. Amazon Fraud Detector
    B. Amazon Comprehend
    C. Amazon Rekognition
    D. Amazon Forecast

18. A model consistently predicts higher risk scores for one demographic
    group because that group was underrepresented in the training data
    relative to its real-world prevalence. Which term describes this
    situation?
    A. Overfitting
    B. Sampling bias
    C. Regularization
    D. Model drift

