---

## Answer key and explanations

1. **B — Generative AI.** The system creates new original content (text,
   images, video) from a prompt rather than classifying or predicting
   from fixed labels, which is the defining trait of generative AI as
   opposed to discriminative models (A), reinforcement learning (C), or a
   fixed rule-based system (D). *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

2. **B — A unit of text that a model processes as a discrete piece of
   input or output.** Tokens are the atomic units — words, sub-words, or
   punctuation — that language models tokenize input into and generate
   output as. An API key (A), a training row (C), and a confidence score
   (D) are unrelated concepts. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

3. **B — Unsupervised learning.** With no predefined labels, the
   algorithm must discover structure in the data on its own, which is
   exactly what clustering-based unsupervised learning does. Supervised
   learning (A) needs labeled outcomes; reinforcement learning (C) needs
   a reward loop; semi-supervised (D) needs at least some labeled data.
   *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

4. **C — Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).** RAG grounds a model's
   responses in an external knowledge source at query time without any
   retraining, which is exactly the "no retraining or fine-tuning"
   constraint here. Continued pre-training (A) and fine-tuning (B) both
   require training; raising temperature (D) affects randomness, not
   grounding. *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation Models)*

5. **B — Explainability.** Explainability is specifically about being
   able to describe or justify why a model produced a given output.
   Robustness (A) concerns reliability under varied input; scalability
   (C) and latency (D) are performance concerns unrelated to
   understanding a specific decision. *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for
   Responsible AI)*

6. **B — Least privilege.** Granting only the permissions strictly
   required for a task, and no more, is the definition of least
   privilege. The shared responsibility model (A) describes the AWS/
   customer security split; defense in depth (C) layers multiple
   controls; data residency (D) concerns where data is stored. *(Domain
   5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI Solutions)*

7. **B — Hallucination.** Generative models can produce fluent,
   confident-sounding output that is factually wrong, a well-documented
   risk teams must mitigate with grounding, guardrails, and human review.
   The other options describe limitations that do not generally apply to
   modern generative AI systems. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative
   AI)*

8. **B — Overfitting.** A large gap between very high training
   performance and much lower test performance is the textbook symptom
   of overfitting, where the model has memorized training data rather
   than learned generalizable patterns. Underfitting (A) would show poor
   performance on both sets; C and D don't match this symptom pattern.
   *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

9. **C — PartyRock, an Amazon Bedrock Playground.** PartyRock is
   purpose-built for no-code, no-infrastructure generative AI app
   prototyping aimed at business users. SageMaker Studio (A) is a
   developer ML IDE; Lambda (B) and EC2 (D) both require writing and
   deploying code or managing servers. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of
   Generative AI)*

10. **B — Embeddings.** Embeddings are dense numeric vectors positioned so
    that semantically similar items land close together in the vector
    space, enabling similarity search. Tokens (A) are discrete text
    units, not vectors; hyperparameters (C) and checkpoints (D) are
    unrelated training artifacts. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative
    AI)*

11. **B — Continued pre-training.** Further unsupervised training on a
    large unlabeled corpus to permanently absorb vocabulary and style,
    without changing overall task capability, is precisely continued
    pre-training. Prompt engineering (A) and zero-shot prompting (D)
    change nothing about the model itself; RAG (C) retrieves data at
    query time rather than absorbing it into weights. *(Domain 3 —
    Applications of Foundation Models)*

12. **C — Historical bias.** Historical bias occurs when the underlying
    real-world data reflects past societal or organizational inequities,
    which the model then learns and perpetuates. Sampling bias (A) is
    about how the dataset was collected relative to the population;
    measurement bias (B) is about how labels/features were measured;
    aggregation bias (D) arises from combining distinct subgroups
    inappropriately. *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

13. **A — AWS Artifact.** AWS Artifact is the self-service portal for
    downloading AWS's independently audited compliance reports (SOC, PCI
    DSS, ISO, and more). CloudTrail (B) logs API activity; Config (C)
    evaluates resource configuration compliance; Macie (D) discovers and
    classifies sensitive data. *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and
    Governance for AI Solutions)*

14. **C — AI is the broadest field; machine learning is a subset of AI;
    deep learning is a subset of machine learning.** This nesting is the
    standard relationship among the three fields. A and D scramble or
    deny that relationship, and B reverses it entirely. *(Domain 1 —
    Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

15. **B — Few-shot prompting.** Providing several example input/output
    pairs in the prompt before the actual task is the definition of
    few-shot prompting. Zero-shot (A) provides no examples; negative
    prompting (C) tells the model what to avoid; retrieval-augmented
    prompting (D) is not a standard term for this technique. *(Domain 2
    — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

16. **B — Latency and cost, weighed against acceptable quality.**
    Foundation model selection is fundamentally a trade-off across
    latency, cost, and quality (along with modality and context window)
    for the specific use case — never one factor in isolation. A, C, and
    D each isolate an irrelevant or overly narrow factor. *(Domain 2 —
    Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

17. **A — Amazon Fraud Detector.** It's a managed service purpose-built to
    detect online fraud patterns (payment fraud, fake accounts) using
    pre-built ML models with minimal setup. Comprehend (B) is text
    analytics, Rekognition (C) is image/video analysis, and Forecast (D)
    is time-series prediction — none purpose-built for fraud detection.
    *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

18. **B — Sampling bias.** When a demographic group is underrepresented in
    training data relative to its real-world prevalence, the resulting
    skewed predictions are a textbook case of sampling bias. Overfitting
    (A) and model drift (D) describe different failure modes;
    regularization (C) is a mitigation technique, not a bias type.
    *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

19. **B — Amazon Bedrock Agents.** Agents are designed to orchestrate
    multi-step tasks autonomously, including calling external APIs and
    summarizing results, without custom orchestration code. Guardrails
    (A) filter content, Model Evaluation (C) scores model quality, and
    Titan Text Embeddings (D) produces vector representations — none
    orchestrate multi-step actions. *(Domain 3 — Applications of
    Foundation Models)*

20. **A — Amazon SageMaker Clarify.** Clarify is specifically built to
    detect bias in data and models and to generate explainability
    reports for individual predictions. Ground Truth (B) is for data
    labeling, CloudWatch (C) is for monitoring/metrics, and Trusted
    Advisor (D) is for cost/security/performance best-practice checks.
    *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

21. **B — Business Associate Addendum (BAA), required for HIPAA
    compliance.** Processing protected health information on AWS
    requires executing a BAA, which extends HIPAA obligations to AWS as
    a business associate. The shared responsibility model (A) is a
    general security framework, not a signed agreement; the Acceptable
    Use Policy (C) and an SLA (D) address unrelated obligations.
    *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI Solutions)*

22. **B — Temperature.** Temperature scales the randomness of the
    probability distribution used to pick the next token — lower values
    concentrate probability on the most likely tokens, producing more
    deterministic output. Context window (A) and max tokens (C) control
    length/scope, not randomness; D incorrectly excludes temperature.
    *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

23. **C — Evaluation and hyperparameter tuning.** In the standard ML
    lifecycle, a trained model is evaluated and tuned before it is
    deployed to production. Business goal identification (A) and data
    collection (B) happen far earlier; monitoring (D) happens only after
    deployment. *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

24. **B — A vector database, such as Amazon OpenSearch Service with a
    vector engine.** Vector databases are purpose-built for storing
    embeddings and performing low-latency similarity search at scale, the
    core requirement of a RAG retrieval step. A relational database with
    only exact-match indexes (A) cannot do similarity search efficiently;
    SQS (C) and KMS (D) are unrelated to vector storage. *(Domain 3 —
    Applications of Foundation Models)*

25. **B — An LLM is a type of foundation model specialized in natural
    language, while "foundation model" is the broader term covering other
    modalities too.** Foundation model is the umbrella category (text,
    image, audio, multimodal); LLM refers specifically to the
    text-focused subset. A, C, and D each state a false or overly rigid
    distinction. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

26. **B and C — Automatic evaluation metrics, using a technique like ROUGE
    score comparison.** Automated, repeatable comparison against
    reference summaries is exactly what automatic metrics like ROUGE (or
    BLEU) provide. Human evaluation panels (A) are valuable but are not
    "automated"; reviewing training code (D) and counting parameters (E)
    don't measure output quality at all. *(Domain 3 — Applications of
    Foundation Models)*

27. **A — Ensuring a model performs reliably and produces factually
    accurate output even under unexpected or adversarial input.**
    Veracity and robustness concern factual correctness and stable
    performance under stress, distinct from fairness (equitable outcomes
    across groups, C), privacy (B), and general security hygiene (D).
    *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

28. **B — AWS CloudTrail.** CloudTrail records a continuous history of API
    calls across an account, including Bedrock and SageMaker API
    activity, supporting security auditing. GuardDuty (A) detects
    threats, Macie (C) classifies sensitive data, and Audit Manager (D)
    assesses compliance posture rather than logging raw API calls.
    *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI Solutions)*

29. **B — A model with high bias tends to underfit; a model with high
    variance tends to overfit.** This is the standard bias–variance
    relationship: high bias means the model is too simple (underfitting),
    while high variance means it's overly sensitive to training data
    noise (overfitting). A, C, and D each misstate the relationship.
    *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

30. **B — Invoke a foundation model through Amazon Bedrock's on-demand or
    provisioned throughput API.** Bedrock is a fully managed API — no
    GPU provisioning, patching, or scaling management required. Self-
    managing EC2 GPUs (A), training from scratch on Trainium (C), and
    building a custom transformer on-premises (D) all require
    significant infrastructure management. *(Domain 3 — Applications of
    Foundation Models)*

31. **B — Amazon Bedrock Guardrails.** Guardrails let you configure
    denied topics, content filters, and word/phrase blocklists that are
    enforced on model input and output regardless of what the underlying
    model would otherwise produce. Knowledge Bases (A) power RAG, the
    Titan Image Generator (C) creates images, and Model Evaluation (D)
    scores quality — none enforce content policy. *(Domain 3 —
    Applications of Foundation Models)*

32. **C — Amazon Textract.** Textract is purpose-built to extract text,
    key-value pairs, and tables while preserving document structure from
    scanned forms and invoices. Comprehend (A) analyzes text that's
    already extracted, Rekognition (B) analyzes images/video generally,
    and Transcribe (D) converts speech to text. *(Domain 1 —
    Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

33. **B — Provisioned Throughput.** Provisioned Throughput lets a
    customer commit to a fixed capacity purchase in exchange for
    guaranteed, consistent throughput and lower latency variance — ideal
    for predictable, high-volume production traffic. On-demand (A) has
    variable capacity; Spot Instances (C) don't apply to Bedrock model
    invocation; Guardrails (D) is a content-safety feature, not a
    capacity model. *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation Models)*

34. **A — Amazon SageMaker Model Cards.** Model Cards are the standardized
    document format for capturing a model's intended use, training data,
    evaluation results, and limitations for governance review. CloudWatch
    Dashboards (B), Cost Explorer (C), and QuickSight (D) address
    monitoring, cost, and BI visualization, not model documentation.
    *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

35. **C — Configuring IAM permissions and classifying the sensitivity of
    the customer's own data.** Under the shared responsibility model,
    "security in the cloud" — including IAM configuration and data
    classification — is always the customer's job. Physical data-center
    security (A), hypervisor patching (B), and storage-hardware
    durability (D) are AWS's responsibility ("security of the cloud").
    *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI Solutions)*

36. **C — Negative prompting.** Explicitly instructing a model to avoid a
    topic or type of content is negative prompting. Chain-of-thought (A)
    asks the model to reason step by step; few-shot (B) provides
    examples; prompt injection (D) is an attack technique, not a
    deliberate engineering choice by the prompt's author. *(Domain 2 —
    Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

37. **B — Retrieval-Augmented Generation with source attribution from the
    retrieved documents.** RAG architectures can return the specific
    source documents used to ground a response, enabling citation and
    verification. Raising max tokens (A), lowering temperature (C), and
    using a smaller model (D) don't provide any mechanism for tracing an
    answer back to a source document. *(Domain 3 — Applications of
    Foundation Models)*

38. **C — Accuracy.** With only 1% positive cases, a model that predicts
    "negative" for every patient would still score about 99% accuracy
    while being clinically useless — the classic accuracy paradox on
    imbalanced data. Recall, precision, and F1 (A, B, D) are all more
    informative for rare-event detection. *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI
    and ML)*

39. **B — Nondeterminism: sampling-based generation combined with
    parameters like temperature introduces variability into token
    selection.** Generative models sample from a probability
    distribution over possible next tokens, so identical prompts can
    yield different outputs unless sampling is constrained to be fully
    deterministic. A, C, and D describe mechanisms that don't exist in
    standard foundation model serving. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of
    Generative AI)*

40. **B — Human evaluation, such as a preference study or rating panel.**
    Subjective quality and user preference require human judgment;
    automatic metrics like BLEU (A) measure lexical overlap, not
    preference, and training loss (C) or parameter count (D) say nothing
    about output quality as perceived by real users. *(Domain 3 —
    Applications of Foundation Models)*

41. **A — A deep neural network may achieve higher accuracy than a linear
    regression model, but its decision process is harder for a human to
    explain.** This captures the classic performance-versus-
    interpretability trade-off: complex models often perform better but
    are harder to explain, while simpler models are easier to interpret
    but may underperform. B, C, and D each deny that a real trade-off
    exists. *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

42. **B — A VPC endpoint (AWS PrivateLink) for Amazon Bedrock.** A VPC
    endpoint keeps traffic between a VPC and Bedrock entirely on the AWS
    network, never traversing the public internet. An internet gateway
    (A), NAT gateway (C), and public Elastic IP (D) all involve public
    internet routing. *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance
    for AI Solutions)*

43. **B — Scalability.** The ability to generate large volumes of
    tailored content on demand, without a linear increase in human
    effort, is a genuine and widely cited advantage of generative AI. A,
    C, and D all describe guarantees generative AI does not actually
    provide. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

44. **B — Amazon Personalize.** Personalize is a fully managed
    recommendation service designed for exactly this use case, requiring
    no in-house ML expertise. SageMaker (A) would require building a
    custom model; Forecast (C) targets time-series prediction; Comprehend
    (D) is for text analytics. *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

45. **A — Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with the pgvector extension.** pgvector
    adds vector similarity search directly to an existing PostgreSQL
    deployment, avoiding a brand-new data store. Kendra (B) is a separate
    enterprise search service, not a relational database replacement;
    DAX (C) accelerates DynamoDB reads and Glue DataBrew (D) is a data
    preparation tool — neither adds vector search to Aurora. *(Domain 3 —
    Applications of Foundation Models)*

46. **A — The ability for operators to constrain, override, or halt a
    model's actions in production.** Controllability is specifically
    about maintaining human oversight and the ability to intervene in a
    deployed system's behavior. B, C, and D describe cost, speed, and
    language-support characteristics unrelated to control. *(Domain 4 —
    Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

47. **C — Reinforcement learning.** An agent that learns optimal actions
    through numeric rewards, with no fixed labeled dataset, is the
    defining pattern of reinforcement learning. Supervised learning (A)
    requires labels; unsupervised learning (B) finds structure without
    rewards; "batch learning" (D) describes a training cadence, not a
    learning paradigm. *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

48. **A — AWS Trainium.** Trainium is AWS's custom silicon purpose-built
    for cost-efficient large-scale model training. Inferentia (B) is
    optimized for inference, not training; Graviton (C) is a general-
    purpose CPU; Nitro (D) is the underlying virtualization system, not
    an ML accelerator. *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation Models)*

49. **A — Disclosing to end users that they are interacting with an AI
    system, rather than a human.** Transparency is about making a
    system's nature and capabilities clear to the people affected by it.
    Encrypting weights (B), maximizing context window (C), and
    minimizing latency (D) are security or performance concerns, not
    transparency. *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

50. **B — GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation).** GDPR governs
    personal data protection and individual privacy rights, including
    the right to erasure, for anyone processing EU residents' data.
    HIPAA (A) covers U.S. health information, PCI DSS (C) covers payment
    card data, and SOC 2 (D) is a security/availability audit standard,
    not a privacy regulation. *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and
    Governance for AI Solutions)*

51. **A — Inferentia is optimized for cost-effective machine learning
    inference; Trainium is optimized for training.** These are AWS's two
    distinct custom ML chip families, each targeting a different stage of
    the ML lifecycle. B, C, and D each misstate their purpose or
    interchange their roles. *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation
    Models)*

52. **C — Customer satisfaction score or cost per resolved support
    ticket.** Business metrics tie model performance to real-world
    organizational outcomes. BLEU (A), ROUGE (B), and perplexity (D) are
    all automatic linguistic metrics that measure text quality, not
    business impact. *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation Models)*

53. **B — A parameter, such as a neural network's learned weights, is
    learned automatically during training; a hyperparameter, such as the
    learning rate, is set before training begins.** This is the standard
    distinction. A reverses it, C denies any difference exists, and D
    incorrectly restricts hyperparameters to unsupervised learning.
    *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

54. **B — Chain-of-thought prompting.** Asking a model to reason through
    intermediate steps before producing a final answer is chain-of-
    thought prompting, and it commonly improves accuracy on multi-step
    problems. Zero-shot (A) provides no reasoning scaffold; prompt
    injection (C) is an attack; negative prompting (D) tells the model
    what to avoid. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

55. **A — Amazon Kendra.** Kendra is a managed enterprise search service
    with built-in connectors (including SharePoint and S3) designed to
    minimize custom integration work. Redshift (B), Glue (C), and EMR
    (D) are data warehousing, ETL, and big-data processing services, not
    purpose-built enterprise search. *(Domain 3 — Applications of
    Foundation Models)*

56. **B — Ensuring personal data used to train or prompt a model is
    protected from unauthorized access and misuse.** This is the core of
    privacy and security as a responsible AI dimension. Equal accuracy
    across groups (A) is fairness; public documentation (C) is
    transparency; carbon footprint (D) is an environmental
    consideration, not privacy/security. *(Domain 4 — Guidelines for
    Responsible AI)*

57. **A — AWS Config.** Config continuously evaluates resource
    configurations against defined rules and flags noncompliant
    resources, such as an unencrypted S3 bucket. Inspector (B) scans for
    vulnerabilities, Trusted Advisor (C) gives general best-practice
    checks, and Detective (D) investigates security findings — none
    perform continuous configuration-compliance evaluation the way
    Config does. *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance for
    AI Solutions)*

58. **B — Supervised learning.** Supervised learning specifically
    requires a labeled dataset with known correct outputs for the model
    to learn to predict. Unsupervised learning (A) has no labels;
    reinforcement learning (C) uses rewards instead of labels;
    "self-organizing learning" (D) is not a standard ML category.
    *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

59. **B — Amazon Q Business.** Q Business is a fully managed assistant
    designed for business users, grounded in a company's own enterprise
    data and systems. Q Developer (A) targets software developers; the
    legacy CodeWhisperer console (C) is a developer tool; Lex (D) builds
    conversational interfaces rather than acting as a ready-made
    enterprise assistant. *(Domain 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI)*

60. **A and C — How much labeled/domain-specific data is available, and
    how much the desired behavior differs from the base model's existing
    capabilities.** These two factors determine where on the spectrum
    (prompt engineering → RAG → fine-tuning → continued pre-training) a
    use case lands: minimal data and small behavior gaps favor prompt
    engineering or RAG, while large data volumes and substantial behavior
    changes favor fine-tuning or continued pre-training. UI color scheme
    (B), AWS Region count (D), and developer operating system (E) are
    irrelevant to this decision. *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation
    Models)*

61. **B — Underfitting.** Poor performance on both the training data and
    the test data indicates the model is too simple to capture the
    underlying pattern at all — underfitting (high bias). Overfitting
    (A) would show a large gap between strong training performance and
    weak test performance, which is not the case here; C and D don't
    match this symptom. *(Domain 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML)*

62. **A — Amazon Q Developer.** Q Developer is purpose-built as an
    AI pair-programming assistant integrated into IDEs, offering code
    completions and explanations. Q Business (B) targets business users
    with enterprise data grounding, while Polly (C) and Transcribe (D)
    handle text-to-speech and speech-to-text, unrelated to coding.
    *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation Models)*

63. **B — Supervised fine-tuning on the labeled example conversations.**
    Fine-tuning on a modest labeled dataset adjusts a model's style and
    behavior for a specific use case while preserving most of its
    general knowledge. Continued pre-training (A) uses unlabeled data at
    much larger scale; prompt engineering alone (C) doesn't durably
    change the model; rebuilding the architecture (D) is neither
    necessary nor practical. *(Domain 3 — Applications of Foundation
    Models)*

64. **A — Uncertainty over intellectual property rights for AI-generated
    content and the provenance of training data.** This is a
    generative-AI-specific legal and ethical question distinct from
    general data privacy, covering questions like who owns generated
    output and whether training data was used with proper rights. B, C,
    and D are unrelated cost or account-configuration details. *(Domain
    4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI)*

65. **A and B — Amazon Macie to discover and classify sensitive data, and
    AWS KMS with customer managed keys (CMKs) to encrypt it.** Macie
    handles automated discovery and classification of sensitive data in
    S3, while KMS with CMKs provides the customer-controlled encryption
    needed for compliance. Personalize (C), Polly (D), and Forecast (E)
    are unrelated AI services with no role in data classification or
    encryption. *(Domain 5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI
    Solutions)*

## Scoring your results

Real AWS exams report a scaled score from 100–1000 with a passing score of
700, computed from a proprietary weighting of scored items (only 50 of the
65 questions on the real exam are scored; 15 are unscored pretest
questions you can't identify). This mock exam doesn't have access to that
scaling model, so use a simpler, more conservative proxy instead:

1. **Overall raw score:** Count your correct answers out of 65.
   - **49/65 (≈75%) or higher:** Roughly comparable to a passing band on
     the real exam. Keep practicing timing and your weakest domain below.
   - **Below 49/65:** Treat this as a signal to revisit the domain guides
     before scheduling the real exam, not just this mock exam again.
2. **Per-domain score:** Using the domain tag on each answer above, tally
   your correct answers within each domain and compare against that
   domain's question count from the [distribution table](#domain-weight-distribution):

   | Domain | Correct / Total |
   |--------|------------------|
   | 1 — Fundamentals of AI and ML | ___ / 13 |
   | 2 — Fundamentals of Generative AI | ___ / 16 |
   | 3 — Applications of Foundation Models | ___ / 18 |
   | 4 — Guidelines for Responsible AI | ___ / 9 |
   | 5 — Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI Solutions | ___ / 9 |

   Because Domain 3 carries the heaviest weight (~28%) on the real exam,
   a weak score there costs you more than a weak score in Domain 4 or 5 —
   prioritize your review accordingly. Revisit the matching
   [domain guide](README.md#exam-domains) for any domain scoring below
   70%, then retake this mock exam after a study pass to check for
   improvement.
